holiday.description:
Buddha Purnima (Buddha Jayanti) is one of the main and the most sacred holidays in Buddhism.

On this day in 563 BC, the Buddha was born, on the same day in 483 BC, he attained enlightenment and left the body after reaching full Nirvana. Three key events in the life of the Buddha are celebrated on the same day.

The word "Buddha" means "awakened" and it is a title. Buddha was born under the name Siddhartha Gautama in a Royal family, in a small country on the border of India and Nepal. Although raised according to his status, he chose not to live in the palace but follow the traditional Indian path of a wandering hermit. This radical decision was prompted by the realization that there are cruel and terrible things in life, such as illness and death, hunger and aging. He went on a journey searching for the meaning of life. However, many years of wandering, learning, meditation and ascetic lifestyle did not reveal the secrets of life and death to him. He decided to change the way to achieve the goal and trust his intuition. Siddhartha sat down under the Bodhi tree and wanted to study himself, his mind, his life experience and look inside his heart. After forty days, on the day of the Full Moon in May, Siddhartha "has awaked", reached complete Nirvana and became the Buddha.

On this important day, big religious ceremonies and chants are held in Buddhist monasteries around the world. People dress up in white clothes and prepare rice pudding — kheer. According to legend, a woman named Sujata once gave kheer to the Buddha on his birthday. Since then, kheer has been a sweet symbol of the holiday.

Put on clean, white clothes and spend the day reading mantras. The most famous mantra in Buddhism is OM MANI PADME HUM, the mantra of compassion and liberation:

OM — removes pride;
MA — removes jealousy;
NI — removes attachments;
PAD — dispels ignorance;
ME — dissolves greed;
HUM — transforms anger.

This great day has very strong energy, look out for the holiday and let the energy fill you.

Kartika or the month of Damodara is the last month of Chaturmasya, a four-month fast (July–November), during which it is conventional to give and fulfill spiritual vows. Kartika month is considered favorable for various spiritual practices.

One of the origin stories says that the month is named after Kartikeya, the second son of Shiva. Kartikeya led the army of Shiva, defeating the demons who once conquered the Universe. Another interpretation says that Damodara is one of the names of Krishna which means one who can be known only via a purified mind. This state can be achieved when a person has self-control — dama.

This month is traditionally celebrated throughout India but the main ceremonies are held in Vrindavan. For followers of Vaishnavism, who especially revere Vishnu and his incarnations, Kartika is one of the most respected and significant months of the year because at this period Almighty shows his mercy to ordinary people.

There are five most important Kartika — gates (vows), the observance of which means the complete fulfillment of the vow:

1. Hari-Jagaranam (the vigil for one and a half an hour before sunrise or the last quarter of the night which must be performed in the temples of Vishnu or Shiva, near Tulasi or seated at the foot of the Ashwattha tree);
2. Offering dipas (burning lamps) to Damodar;
3. Morning bath in a holy place;
4. The Tulasi worship;
5. The holiday of Mahotsova at the end of Kartika.

In order to propitiate the Almighty, it is necessary to offer the Lord a wick with ghee or a small lamp, every day, and also to sing Damodarastakam — a sacred prayer to Lord Damodara. During this period, give spiritual vows. For example, you can choose one of your problems and using the strength and influence of Kartika, try to change the situation, hoping for help and support from higher powers.
